# The Parquet store and logs are generated artefacts, never committed.
#
# This is the rule worth defending. The store will grow to hundreds of megabytes
# of binary data that changes on every run, and git handles that badly: it cannot
# diff it, cannot compress successive versions of it, and once a large file is in
# the history it is there permanently. The store is also entirely reproducible --
# that is the whole point of this project -- so committing it stores no
# information that the code does not already contain.
data/
logs/

.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# macOS writes these into any folder it previews. Harmless, but they turn up in
# every `git status` forever if not ignored.
.DS_Store

# Deliberately NOT ignored, in case a future search suggests otherwise:
#
# uv.lock          The exact versions every dependency resolved to. Committing it
#                  is what makes `uv sync` reproduce this environment rather than
#                  a similar one. Many .gitignore templates found online exclude
#                  lockfiles; that advice is for libraries, not applications.
# config/          Small, hand-edited, and carries the reasoning behind the
#                  exchange choice. It is input, not output.
# .vscode/         The debug and test settings that make the project work in the
#                  editor. Committing them means a fresh checkout works without
#                  reconstructing them from memory.
